# Line-ending policy (Phase 1.8C, ADR-008).
#
# The repository stores LF for every text file — this was already true for all
# tracked files before this file existed, so introducing it rewrites nothing.
# Text files also check out as LF on every platform. The project is
# Windows-primary, but every tool in the stack (Python, Ruff, MyPy, Pytest,
# modern editors) handles LF transparently, and a single working-tree encoding
# means test fixtures and hashed artifacts have identical bytes on Windows and
# Ubuntu CI. Only formats that genuinely expect CRLF (Windows shell scripts)
# are checked out as CRLF.
#
# Developer-facing consequences:
# - Git's "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings disappear for governed files:
#   explicit attributes override core.autocrlf.
# - Working trees checked out before this file keep their existing endings
#   until a file is re-checked out; that skew is cosmetic and does not show up
#   in `git status` or diffs, because comparison happens on normalized content.
#   A fresh clone, or `git checkout -- <path>` on a file, yields the declared
#   endings.

# Default: auto-detect text, store LF, check out LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit text types (documentation of intent; all inherit LF).
*.py      text eol=lf
*.pyi     text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.csv     text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.env.example   text eol=lf

# Windows shell scripts are the one genuine CRLF case: cmd.exe mis-parses some
# LF-only batch constructs, and CRLF is the PowerShell convention.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Binary: never translated, never diffed as text.
*.parquet binary
*.dbn     binary
*.zst     binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.svg     text eol=lf
*.zip     binary
*.gz      binary
*.tar     binary
*.7z      binary
*.whl     binary
